WebKit: the curl NetworkCache::Data can share a mapped content rule list with another process.

A content rule list (ENABLE_CONTENT_EXTENSIONS, LazyWeb+'s ad blocker, issue #845) is compiled
into a file, mapped, and handed to the WebProcess and the NetworkProcess as shared memory:
API::createExtension (APIContentRuleListStore.cpp) asks the mapped NetworkCache::Data for
tryCreateSharedMemory(). The curl backend defines that only for Windows (a file mapping
handle), so WebKit's UI process did not link with content extensions on.

On Unix-domain-socket platforms (LazyOS) the content rule list store maps its files with
mapFileForSharing, which keeps the file's read-only descriptor with the map, and
tryCreateSharedMemory wraps both as SharedMemory (wrapMap neither unmaps nor closes them;
createHandle sends a duplicate of the read-only descriptor, so no receiver can write the list).
Every other map closes its descriptor in adoptMap as upstream does: an earlier version of this
patch kept it in every mapped Data, so a NetworkProcess held one descriptor per cache entry it
had mapped (issue #892).

The wrapper must not outlive the Data whose map it names. API::ContentRuleList is the one owner
of both (WebCompiledContentRuleList, holding the SharedMemory, has no other owner in the UI
process; the copies of its data made to send it to a process are encoded at once, which
duplicates the descriptor, and dropped), and its members are reordered so the compiled list is
destroyed before the mapped file.

